WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.134
- Somebody, please!

00:00:02.134 --> 00:00:03.690
Get in here now!

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:04.980
- Is this Ebola?

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:06.160
Is this Ebola?

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.330
Hey guys, since we're all in quarantine

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:10.490
because of this coronavirus pandemic,

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:11.960
I thought there was no better time

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:16.260
to watch a "House, M.D."
episode about a quarantine.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:17.311
- [Mom] Can I see?

00:00:17.311 --> 00:00:19.030
(glass crashes)

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:19.943
- Ow!
- Julie!

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:23.350
- Get her a towel.

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:28.260
What was in that jar?
(suspenseful music)

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.840
- This acting is awful.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:32.440
- Smallpox was eradicated
over 30 years ago.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:33.880
- So were Hush Puppies,
have you checked out

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.660
your local hipster coffee shop lately?

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:38.010
- Smallpox doesn't
exist outside of P4 labs

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:39.070
and bio-weapons plants.

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:40.610
- So that's actually true.

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:42.610
I believe there's only four labs,

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:44.170
or four locations in the world,

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:48.010
that have samples of smallpox
still available for testing,

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:50.320
because we felt like there would still be

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:51.870
worthwhile evidence that we can collect

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.360
from how this virus spreads,
how we can eradicate it.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.170
We've limited because of
the fear of bio-terrorism.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:59.830
- She was in Bermuda.
- Or she was scuba diving

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:01.760
on the wreck of a Dutch slave ship

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.440
that had been intentionally sunk

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:05.890
because an epidemic of smallpox
had broken out on board.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:07.880
- House always presents himself to know

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:10.040
these just absurd things.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:13.140
How does he know that she
was in this shipwreck?

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:14.490
I want to be in the room where he's

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:15.830
questioning his patients.

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:17.080
When a patient comes into my office

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:18.390
and they have high fever or vomiting,

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:22.010
I don't ask if they've been
on a pirate ship lately.

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:23.960
I just can't imagine how he extracts

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:24.960
that bit of information.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.330
- Thanks for calling back.

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:29.980
Listen, I'm a screenwriter, and
is it theoretically possible

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:33.200
for smallpox to survive
for more than 200 years

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.435
underwater in a sealed glass jar?

00:01:36.435 --> 00:01:39.160
- [CDC Doctor] Well,
theoretically yes, but--

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:39.993
- Thank you.
(phone clicks)

00:01:39.993 --> 00:01:41.920
- Can you imagine, you just call the CDC

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:43.560
and ask random questions like that?

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.470
- Isolate, get baseline
blood draws for smallpox,

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:48.550
antibodies, and vaccinate
the entire family.

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:51.470
- Okay, first of all, I
can't imagine what lab

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:53.930
is gonna do smallpox testing nowadays.

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:56.300
It has to be a send-in test to Atlanta,

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:58.630
where the CDC headquarters are.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:00.570
I don't have tests for that in my office.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:03.100
Especially on my Epic, the
electronic medical record,

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.780
I think if I put in
smallpox there's gonna be

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:07.600
some major alert to tell
me to contact the CDC.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.077
- We should test for the
21st century suspects.

00:02:10.077 --> 00:02:11.670
Varicella, measles.

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.800
- You're free to perform
whatever unnecessary tests

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.360
you want, Foreman.
- In all honesty,

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.313
Foreman here is 100,000% correct.

00:02:18.313 --> 00:02:19.146
(bell dings)

00:02:19.146 --> 00:02:21.440
If a young woman presents to the office

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.630
with high fever, vomiting
and bloodshot eyes,

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:26.430
you should rule out everything
that it's most likely to be.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:28.640
Well, first, anything that's
most likely to be deadly.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.360
Smallpox should be way, way, way low

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.300
on the list of differential diagnoses.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:34.880
When I think about that old-school saying,

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.770
when you hear hoof beats
you should think horses,

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:38.930
not zebras, because thinking about zebras,

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:41.130
you'll miss some pretty
important cases that are

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.598
easily treatable, like the
influenza virus, like measles.

00:02:44.598 --> 00:02:47.180
(door hisses)

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:49.330
- Why the hell do you guys
have us quarantined in here?

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:51.240
- We're just taking every precaution.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:52.073
- Against what?

00:02:52.073 --> 00:02:53.900
- So I think this is a
great time to explain

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.580
the difference between
isolation and quarantine.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.350
Isolation is when you
take someone who is sick,

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:02.860
and diagnosed sick with a
specific virus or bacteria,

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:05.070
and you keep them isolated
from other people.

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:06.780
There's different forms of isolation,

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:09.290
droplet, airborne, contact, what have you.

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:11.250
Then, if you're wondering if someone

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.180
has potentially been exposed by something,

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:14.610
they may or may not be sick,

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:17.370
they can be held in
quarantine, not isolation.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:19.560
That's what I'm doing here,
I'm technically in quarantine,

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.335
'cause I'm not sick, I may
or may not become sick,

00:03:22.335 --> 00:03:25.570
but that's what we're all
doing, social distancing.

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:27.260
- That does not look like tetanus shots.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.060
- It's really not worth going into

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:30.883
because the odds are astronomical.

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:34.523
- Tell me.

00:03:34.523 --> 00:03:36.830
(suspenseful music)

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:38.653
- Again, it's--
- Smallpox.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:41.490
- Smallpox?

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.447
- That would've been really
awesome if she'd just said,

00:03:43.447 --> 00:03:44.367
"You know what you should do?

00:03:44.367 --> 00:03:46.320
"You should stay alert, not anxious."

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:47.390
I would've been really proud of

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:49.130
the "House, M.D." show for doing that.

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:50.630
- Negative for varicella and measles,

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:53.020
so all of our ideas are off the table.

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:54.270
- Well, I'm putting mine back on.

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:56.220
Patient's a diver.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.210
Under increased pressure,
everything in the blood

00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:00.580
gets scared, panics, runs and hides.

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:02.030
- Antibodies could be in the joints.

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:04.010
- Tap her knee, ankle, shoulder

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:06.520
and any other joint in
her smallpox-ridden body.

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:10.210
- Doing a joint aspiration to check

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:13.430
if it is in fact smallpox
seems kind of dangerous.

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:16.700
There are true times when you
need to do a joint aspiration,

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:18.480
like if a patient has septic arthritis

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.540
and you need to culture the joint fluid

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:22.400
to see what antibiotics you should use,

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:24.260
or if you're looking for gout.

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:26.510
But just to do it on the
off-chance that she may

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:28.690
or may not have smallpox,
that actually puts the patient

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:30.430
at risk for septic arthritis, which is

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:32.180
a bacterial infection of the joint.

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:36.153
Oh no, what does he see?
- What?

00:04:37.449 --> 00:04:38.366
What is it?

00:04:40.486 --> 00:04:43.401
- I'm sorry, I have to call the CDC.

00:04:43.401 --> 00:04:44.440
- I don't know what he saw.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.063
- I think you do have smallpox.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.870
- Blisters?
- Whoa, take a look at this.

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:51.620
- What is it?

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:53.770
- There's a rash under
your daughter's arm.

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:57.303
- Like more of those gross bumps?

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.471
- No, it's just a regular rash.

00:05:02.471 --> 00:05:03.893
- What does that mean?

00:05:05.233 --> 00:05:07.260
- It means it's not smallpox.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:08.240
- I don't know why not,

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:11.120
'cause that still could
be a smallpox rash.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:12.470
House, explain it to me!

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:16.410
- I'm Dr. Broda, head of
Infection Control, CDC,

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:18.290
and your protective
suits are not adequate.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:20.200
- Turns out they don't have to be.

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:22.710
Rash under the arm
means it's not smallpox.

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:25.230
False alarm, sorry.

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:26.510
Hope the traffic wasn't too bad.

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:28.230
- Rash is consistent with smallpox.

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:29.950
- Not if it presents after pustules.

00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:32.180
- She's probably just
allergic to the bedsheets

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:34.410
or the hospital gown and
you simply didn't notice it.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:37.290
We're gonna airlift blood and
tissue samples to Atlanta.

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:39.640
We'll have the DNA results in 18 hours.

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:42.590
In the meantime, please
step out of the room.

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:45.500
- It's funny I find myself
agreeing with the CDC doctor

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:46.830
more than I am with Dr. House,

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:48.763
but I know it's probably to my demise.

00:05:49.857 --> 00:05:52.357
(eerie music)

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.500
- Sir, are you feeling all right in there?

00:05:57.500 --> 00:05:58.570
- It's just a headache!

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:00.023
- Oh no, his eyes.

00:06:02.595 --> 00:06:03.428
- Baby.

00:06:05.328 --> 00:06:06.623
Baby!

00:06:06.623 --> 00:06:08.070
- [Dad] Oh my god.

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:10.140
- Dad, you're bleeding!

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:12.190
- Somebody, please!

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:13.680
Get in here now!

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:14.943
- Is this Ebola?

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:17.783
Is this Ebola?

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:20.240
- Why have they stopped?

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:24.180
- You can't go out there.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:25.320
- Why did you stop?

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:26.620
- Get out of the hallway!

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:28.590
- He does not have smallpox.

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:30.203
- Yes, he does.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:31.953
- It itches!

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:34.970
- He's just developed pustules.

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:37.010
He's now too dangerous to transport.

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:40.040
- The virus that causes
smallpox is called variola.

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:43.700
And before we had vaccinations
we used to do variolation,

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:46.670
where you take a piece of the
pustule that people develop

00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:49.600
and you introduce it to somebody
in order to inoculate them,

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:51.550
meaning to get their immune system ready

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:53.310
to fact the real smallpox.

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:56.024
That wasn't as good as the
vaccines we have today,

00:06:56.024 --> 00:06:59.560
because you actually got sick
with smallpox at that case.

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:01.980
However, your case wasn't as bad as those

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:03.580
who caught it naturally.

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:05.530
So this was a way to get sick,

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:07.730
but not as sick as everyone else.

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:09.760
It was only until we
got the smallpox vaccine

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:11.800
where we were truly able to eradicate

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:13.253
smallpox from the world.

00:07:15.737 --> 00:07:16.570
- Uh-uh, wait.

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:21.210
The soles of your feet,
there aren't any lesions.

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:23.790
- No pustules on the daughter's palms, so?

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:25.266
- So therefore your theory is

00:07:25.266 --> 00:07:27.010
you asked me to take the dad's blood

00:07:27.010 --> 00:07:30.040
but I accidentally
injected him with smallpox.

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:30.970
- Exactly.

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:33.080
Although technically it
was the vaccinia virus,

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:35.150
which is what the smallpox
vaccine is made from.

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:38.390
Same symptoms as smallpox,
but only half the calories

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:39.850
and almost none of the lethality.

00:07:39.850 --> 00:07:41.710
- You sound like one of
those anti-vaccine cranks.

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:43.070
You can't develop full-blown symptoms

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:44.080
from the vaccine this quickly.

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:45.980
- You can if you're immunocompromised.

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:49.347
Am I the only one who reads these things?

00:07:49.347 --> 00:07:51.410
Dad had kidney cancer.
- Six years ago.

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:53.250
He's been in remission.
- Well, it's obviously back.

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:54.850
- I swear, House just walks around

00:07:54.850 --> 00:07:57.800
and throws diagnoses on
everyone that could be possible.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:00.480
And I feel like he does
this so inaccurately,

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:03.130
that he has such a low success
rate, it doesn't matter,

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:04.480
'cause at the end of the day, each time

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:06.830
he gets proven wrong, he'll
figure out something else

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:08.750
that he can throw out
there, and he'll be right.

00:08:08.750 --> 00:08:10.940
Not a fan of this approach,
not very realistic.

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:13.110
- You got any proof the
kidney cancer's back?

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:14.023
- Well, let me give him this.

00:08:14.023 --> 00:08:16.790
When he gets better that'll
be the proof you need.

00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:18.710
I'll put on one of your fancy spacesuits

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:21.370
and I promise I won't
kiss him on an open sore.

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:22.750
- You know who Janet Parker is?

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:25.250
- That's the last person
who died of smallpox!

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:29.690
- In 1978 she was working
in a university in England.

00:08:29.690 --> 00:08:32.590
Someone in a research lab
on a floor below screwed up.

00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:36.350
Some smallpox virus managed
to float up through the vents,

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:39.200
into the room where she was working.

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:41.470
She died four days later.

00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:44.330
Last known person to die from smallpox.

00:08:44.330 --> 00:08:49.010
And the person in charge
of the lab was so destroyed

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:51.210
he killed himself.
- Wow.

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:53.830
Didn't know all that.
- Now that he's shedding,

00:08:53.830 --> 00:08:55.720
I can't open that door for anyone.

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:57.230
- When he says, "Now that he's shedding,"

00:08:57.230 --> 00:08:59.190
he's talking about the pustules.

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:02.720
That is the most infectious
stage of smallpox,

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:05.140
so you don't want to be
anywhere near a person

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:07.210
if they truly do have it.

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:08.807
- Hey!
- Oh no, House!

00:09:08.807 --> 00:09:09.930
(lock beeps)

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:10.763
- [Broda] Hey!

00:09:10.763 --> 00:09:13.020
- This is textbook smallpox.

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:15.570
DDXing at this point, it
isn't gonna make a difference.

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:17.120
- Don't say that.
- I'm sorry.

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:22.871
House, you screwed up going in there.

00:09:22.871 --> 00:09:26.930
(EKG screeching)
(somber music)

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:28.740
- Chest compressions, chest compressions,

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:29.833
chest compressions.

00:09:35.685 --> 00:09:38.270
Even House fails us with
the chest compressions.

00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:40.450
He's starting to get
the defibrillator ready

00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:41.930
instead of doing chest compressions.

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:43.750
I know he's out by himself, but still.

00:09:43.750 --> 00:09:45.140
And I'm pretty sure the rhythm I saw

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:46.870
pop up there was flatline.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:48.840
You don't shock flatline either, House.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:51.880
You know better than that.
- Did the cat get sick?

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:53.510
- [Chris] Smallpox is exclusive to humans.

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:56.100
- Is this toxoplasmosis?
- Captains didn't have pets.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:57.660
Nothing and nobody went on those ships

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:00.430
if it didn't have a purpose.
- Hunting mice and rats?

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:04.450
- For the crew, the hold was
probably infested with mice.

00:10:04.450 --> 00:10:06.190
What if that's the difference?

00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:07.850
- What happened to Hairrot?

00:10:09.574 --> 00:10:10.550
- Aw!

00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:13.253
- Is that a sad aw or a cute aw?

00:10:13.253 --> 00:10:15.693
- It said that he dies, it's a sad aw.

00:10:18.410 --> 00:10:20.310
- Before he died, did he lose his fur?

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:25.393
- How did you know?

00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:28.760
- Mice don't carry smallpox,

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:30.410
but they do carry rickettsialpox.

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:33.030
- My knowledge of rickettsialpox
is not up to date.

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:35.340
- Rickettsialpox is mild, self-limited,

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:37.760
zoonotic febrile illness characterized

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:40.800
by eschar formation at the
location of a mite bite,

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:42.760
following the onset of systemic symptoms

00:10:42.760 --> 00:10:45.490
and a more generalized
papulovesicular rash.

00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:48.250
Rickettsialpox occurs
primarily in urban areas,

00:10:48.250 --> 00:10:50.660
where density of mites,
mice and humans is high.

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:54.670
They named it in 1946 in New
York City, shout out NYC.

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:59.050
In about 2007 there's
been 14 cases reported.

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:02.440
Approximately 1.7 cases
per million people.

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:03.840
That's why I haven't seen it.

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:05.380
- At least look at the dad's body.

00:11:05.380 --> 00:11:08.533
Small patches of black dead
tissue will prove I'm right.

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:13.380
Eschars appear with rickettsialpox,
but not with smallpox.

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:15.826
- If he had developed
eschars I would've noticed.

00:11:15.826 --> 00:11:18.826
(suspenseful music)

00:11:20.266 --> 00:11:21.099
- Eschar!

00:11:26.075 --> 00:11:26.908
Eschars.

00:11:28.150 --> 00:11:30.750
- Eschar's just dead tissue on the body.

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:32.710
You frequently see it with abscesses,

00:11:32.710 --> 00:11:34.760
meaning when someone has
a pocket of infection.

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:37.950
But it's very common,
appearing of where a patient

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:42.240
was bitten by a mite in R-pox.

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:45.671
- Start the girl on doxycycline right now.

00:11:45.671 --> 00:11:48.171
(door hisses)

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:54.030
- I still wouldn't take
off my gear like that!

00:11:54.030 --> 00:11:55.970
Oh my god, just 'cause he saw,

00:11:55.970 --> 00:11:58.200
that eschar coulda been
caused by anything.

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:00.450
My god, this is ridiculous.

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:03.170
Infection control people,
this would never happen

00:12:03.170 --> 00:12:05.260
in any hospital, ever, ever, ever.

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:08.420
If this truly happened in
this manner in a hospital,

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:10.620
House would still be kept under quarantine

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:13.490
just in case he didn't have
another infectious condition.

00:12:13.490 --> 00:12:16.240
Because god forbid you
let him out too early,

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:17.810
and now he's walking around infecting

00:12:17.810 --> 00:12:20.150
all the future patients he
sees and his colleagues.

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:21.090
No way.

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:22.430
No, no way.

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:24.320
If you're a fan of "House,
M.D.," I put together

